Fall back to WebGL2 when a browser's WebGPU canvases show nothing.

What: gpui_web's automatic backend choice (`WebBackendPreference::Auto`) gets one more WebGPU check after the device is
made: `webgpu_canvas_shows_drawing` clears a detached 4x4 WebGPU canvas to red and reads it back through a 2D
`OffscreenCanvas` in the same task. Anything but opaque red counts as WebGPU failing, so the existing fallback runs:
the canvas is replaced, `WebGPU initialization failed; falling back to WebGL2: WebGPU draws nothing to a canvas here`
is logged, and the window draws with WebGL2. An app that asks for WebGPU explicitly is not probed.

Why: Chromium 158 (Fedora's build) on NVIDIA's proprietary Linux driver (RTX 5060, Wayland) gives a working NVIDIA
WebGPU adapter whose canvases composite black whenever Chromium's Vulkan feature is on (`--enable-features=Vulkan`,
with `--use-angle=vulkan`, headed or headless), and its default flags give a SwiftShader adapter that does the same:
a ten-line page clearing a WebGPU canvas red shows black, and drawImage of it reads 0,0,0,0. GPUI then showed an
all-black window (seen on the owner's desktop, 2026-11-28). The same read gives 255,0,0,255 where WebGPU works
(`--use-angle=gl`). The choice is private to gpui_web's platform (no extension point).

Tests: in a browser (docs/native-view-web.md, WebGPU): with `--enable-features=Vulkan --use-angle=vulkan` hello and
native-view-web log the fallback and draw with WebGL2; with `--use-angle=gl` they keep WebGPU.

Upstream PR: not filed (filing paused, patch rule 14).

